// ora-mod-gl — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-gl-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
// Oracle Modules — General Ledger deep dive (part 1: Fusion GL). Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB["ora-mod-gl"] = (QB["ora-mod-gl"] || []).concat([
["Fusion GL", "Walk me through the key components you set up for a ledger in Fusion GL.", "**Why they ask:** the ledger is the foundation of Fusion Financials. They want to hear that you've designed one, not just used one.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I think of a ledger as the four Cs: chart of accounts, calendar, currency and convention, meaning the accounting method. So first the chart of accounts structure and instance, with segment labels for primary balancing, cost center, natural account and intercompany. Then the accounting calendar, with an adjusting period for year-end. Then the ledger currency and the SLA accounting method. Then I assign legal entities and their balancing segment values. Then the ledger options: retained earnings, suspense, rounding, CTA and net income accounts, entered currency balancing, journal approval, intercompany balancing, revaluation and translation defaults, and average balances if needed. After that come reporting currencies or secondary ledgers, ledger sets and data access sets, and finally the account hierarchies published to the balances cube.\"\n\n- **The four Cs:** chart of accounts, calendar, currency, accounting method.\n- **Legal entities:** and their balancing segment values.\n- **Ledger options:** retained earnings, suspense, rounding, CTA, approvals, intercompany.\n- **Extensions:** reporting currencies, secondary ledgers, ledger sets, data access sets.\n- **Reporting:** hierarchies published to the cube.\n\nTip: \"The four Cs\" is an easy hook that interviewers remember. Then walk the options in order.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["Which of the four Cs is hardest to change after go-live, and why?","The chart of accounts structure. Segments and their order are fixed once the ledger has transactions, so adding a segment later means a new COA and a new ledger. The calendar is close behind, because you can add future periods but not change existing ones."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What is the GL balances cube (Essbase) and why does it matter?", "**Why they ask:** the cube drives almost all Fusion GL reporting. When it's wrong, every report is wrong.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Every chart of accounts and calendar combination gets an Essbase balances cube, which updates in real time when journals post. Smart View, Financial Reporting, Account Monitor, Account Inspector and Calculation Manager allocations all read it. Its dimensions are accounting period, ledger, each COA segment, currency, currency type, scenario like actual or budget, balance amount like period, quarter or year to date, and amount type. Account hierarchies have to be published to the cube for roll-ups to work. If it gets out of sync, for example after a hierarchy change, I run Create Cubes, Rebuild Dimension Members or Refresh, depending on what changed.\"\n\nTip: Mention the fix when it's out of sync. Support teams spend real time on that.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What happens to reporting if the balances cube is out of date?","Smart View, Financial Reporting and allocations show stale numbers while the trial balance report is correct, which looks like a mismatch. I'd check the Update Balances Cube process and rerun it for the ledger and periods affected."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How does year-end close work in Fusion GL?", "**Why they ask:** year-end is high pressure, and many people think they need a closing journal.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I post all year-end adjustments, including in the adjusting period if the calendar has one. I run the final revaluation, translation, intercompany and allocations, and reconcile. Then I open the first period of the new year. At that moment GL automatically rolls the P&L balances into the retained earnings account defined on the ledger, by balancing segment, and carries balance sheet balances forward. No closing journal is needed. If a late adjustment hits the prior year after the new year is open, I reopen the prior period, post it, and reopen the first period of the new year to refresh retained earnings. After the audit, the prior year is closed or permanently closed.\"\n\nTip: Say \"no closing journal is needed\" clearly. That's the point they're testing.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["Where does retained earnings come from if you have multiple balancing segment values?","Fusion rolls P&L into the retained earnings account for each primary balancing segment value separately, so each legal entity or company gets its own retained earnings balance. You define the account in the ledger options and the balancing value comes from the P&L lines."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What are the journal reversal options in Fusion GL?", "**Why they ask:** accrual reversals are monthly work. They want to see you can automate them.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The reversal period can be the next period, the same period, or a specific period, and average balance ledgers also need a reversal date. The method is either switch debit and credit, or change sign, which posts negative amounts. I set reversal criteria by journal category in Manage Journal Reversal Criteria, so for example all accrual journals reverse automatically in the next period, and schedule AutoReverse. Users can still reverse a journal by hand from the journal page. Either way, the reversal stays linked to the original for audit.\"\n\nTip: Mention setting it by category. Automating accrual reversals is the practical win.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Why would you choose change sign over switch debit and credit?","Change sign keeps the reversal on the same side of the account with negative amounts, so the gross debits and credits aren't inflated. Companies that analyze activity, not just balances, often prefer it; it's set per category in the reversal criteria."]}],
["Fusion GL", "Explain reporting currency conversion levels: subledger, journal and balance.", "**Why they ask:** choosing the wrong level costs performance or detail. They want a reasoned choice.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Balance level converts period-end balances, like translation. It's the lightest, but you get no transaction detail in the reporting currency. Journal level converts every posted GL journal into the reporting currency, so you can see journals but not subledger detail. Subledger level has SLA create entries directly in the reporting currency for every subledger transaction. It gives the most detail but the most volume. I choose based on what the business needs to see: if the local tax authority needs invoice-level detail in a second currency, subledger level; if the group just needs a converted trial balance, balance level. The same three levels apply to secondary ledgers.\"\n\nTip: Tie the choice to a business need. That's what makes it a design answer.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Which conversion level would you pick for a group that needs EUR reporting with drill-down to invoices?","Subledger level, because it's the only one that keeps subledger detail in the reporting currency. If they just need EUR balances for consolidation, balance level is lighter and cheaper to maintain."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What are journal conversion rules and COA mappings for secondary ledgers?", "**Why they ask:** secondary ledgers fail if these aren't designed carefully.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Journal conversion rules decide which journals, by source and category, are copied from the primary to the secondary ledger at journal level. For example, I'd exclude manual IFRS-only adjustments so they don't land in the US GAAP ledger. A chart of accounts mapping converts primary account combinations into the secondary ledger's chart, using segment rules or account rules. That's needed when the secondary ledger uses a different chart, like a local statutory chart in France. Both are set on the relationship between the primary and secondary ledger.\"\n\nTip: Give the French statutory chart example. It's the most common real case.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What goes wrong if the COA mapping misses a new account?","Journals that use the unmapped account fail to replicate or post to the secondary ledger, so the two ledgers drift apart. I add new values to the mapping as part of the account-creation process and check replication exceptions each close."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How do you set up revaluation in Fusion GL?", "**Why they ask:** revaluation is a monthly step for any multi-currency company.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I define the revaluation with the accounts to revalue, usually monetary balance sheet accounts held in foreign currency: AR, AP, cash, intercompany and loans. I set the rate type, often a period-end Corporate rate, the unrealized gain and loss accounts, and whether to revalue P&L accounts as well. I can keep the primary balancing segment from the source account so the gain or loss lands in the right entity. It runs per ledger or ledger set each period, I post the journal it creates, and that journal usually reverses automatically next period.\"\n\nTip: Say which accounts are monetary. It shows you understand the accounting behind it.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["Why don't you revalue P&L accounts in most cases?","P&L items are already recorded at the transaction-date rate, which is the correct measure under ASC 830 and IAS 21. Revaluing them would distort period results; only some companies revalue them to keep YTD balances at a current rate for management reporting."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How does translation work in Fusion GL and where does CTA go?", "**Why they ask:** translation is core to consolidation, and CTA confuses many people.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Translation converts a ledger's balances, not its journals, into another currency for reporting. Balance sheet accounts use the period-end rate, income statement accounts use the average rate for the period, and equity and other historical accounts use historical rates or amounts, set up in Manage Historical Rates. Because different rates are used, the translated trial balance doesn't balance, and that difference goes to the cumulative translation adjustment account defined on the ledger. I run translation after revaluation and all postings, and rerun it after any late adjustment. Many groups now translate in FCCS and use GL translation only for local needs.\"\n\nTip: Explain why CTA exists: different rates for different accounts. That's the real question.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["Why do equity accounts use historical rates?","Equity like common stock and opening retained earnings should stay at the rate when the capital was contributed or the profit was earned. Translating at today's rate would create artificial movements; the difference is captured in CTA instead."]}],
["Fusion GL", "Explain how intercompany balancing works when a journal crosses legal entities.", "**Why they ask:** intercompany is one of the hardest parts of a multi-entity close.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"If a journal has lines for different primary balancing segment values, meaning different legal entities, Fusion adds intercompany receivable and payable lines automatically so each entity balances. The rules come from Manage Intercompany Balancing Rules, by ledger, legal entity, source, category or chart of accounts. For example, a rule might say use account 1810 for intercompany receivables and put the trading partner's balancing value in the intercompany segment. If there are many entities on one side, clearing company options decide which one balances against which. Intracompany balancing handles balancing values inside the same legal entity. These lines then feed intercompany reconciliation and eliminations.\"\n\nTip: Mention the intercompany segment holding the partner. It's what makes reconciliation and elimination work.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you decide between intercompany and intracompany balancing?","Intercompany balancing applies across legal entities and creates receivable and payable lines between them. Intracompany applies between balancing segment values inside one legal entity, like two divisions. Both use rules in Manage Intercompany Balancing Rules; I configure clearing companies for intracompany if they want one."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What is a suspense account and when would you enable it?", "**Why they ask:** a basic control question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"If a journal doesn't balance, GL can post the difference to a suspense account instead of rejecting it. It's enabled per ledger, and you can have different suspense accounts by source and category. Most companies leave it off for manual journals and integrations, because it's better to fix the source than to hide the error. If it is on, the suspense balance has to be reconciled and cleared every period, because auditors will ask about it.\"\n\nTip: Say you'd generally keep it off. It shows control thinking.", "B", null, null, {"f":["If suspense is off and a large integration journal won't balance, what do you do?","I find the cause in the source file, usually rounding, a missing line or a currency conversion issue, and fix it at the source. Posting to suspense hides the problem and leaves someone to clean it up later."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How do you load budgets into Fusion GL?", "**Why they ask:** budget vs actual reporting is something every finance team wants.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Budgets live as scenarios in the balances cube. I can load them by spreadsheet through Smart View or the budget upload, or with the FBDI budget template, by account and period. For a real planning process, EPM Planning is better, and it sends the approved budget into GL. Once loaded, budget vs actual reports run in Financial Reporting or Smart View. Budgetary control budgets are a different thing: they're control budgets that check and block spending, not just report against it.\"\n\nTip: Separate reporting budgets from control budgets. Interviewers often follow up on it.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you make budget vs actual reports work by department?","The budget must be loaded at the same segment level as actuals, at least to cost center and natural account, using the same calendar and ledger. Then Smart View or Financial Reporting compares the Budget scenario with Actual using the same POV."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What are statistical journals and statistical accounts used for?", "**Why they ask:** statistics drive allocations, and allocations drive management reporting.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"They record non-money quantities in the ledger, like headcount, square footage or units produced, either in the STAT currency or in statistical accounts. The main use is as a basis for allocations, for example allocating rent by square feet or IT cost by headcount, and for KPIs like cost per employee. They're loaded each period by journal, spreadsheet or integration, often from HR for headcount.\"\n\nTip: Give the rent by square feet example. It's the classic use.", "B", null, null, {"f":["Why might you keep statistical data outside the ledger instead?","If headcount or square footage already lives in HCM or a facilities system, loading it monthly as statistical journals can duplicate effort. But for allocations in Calculation Manager it needs to be in the cube, so I'd automate the load rather than key it."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How do allocations work in Fusion GL with Calculation Manager?", "**Why they ask:** allocations replace a lot of spreadsheet work at month-end.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Allocation rules are built in Calculation Manager against the balances cube. Each rule has a source, which is the pool to allocate, like total IT cost; a basis, which is the driver, like headcount by department; a target, the accounts and cost centers that receive it; and an offset, which credits the pool back. Rule sets run several rules in order for step-down allocations. Runtime prompts for period and ledger make the rules reusable every month. Generate General Ledger Allocations creates the journals, which can post automatically. I always test with known numbers first and document the drivers for audit.\"\n\nTip: Walk source, basis, target, offset in that order. It's the structure they expect.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you test an allocation before running it for real?","I run it in a test environment or as a draft and validate that the total allocated equals the source pool and the offset nets it to zero. Then I check a few target lines by hand against the driver percentages before scheduling it monthly."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How do you troubleshoot a Smart View report that doesn't match the trial balance?", "**Why they ask:** this happens constantly during close. They want a method.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I check the point of view: ledger, period, currency, currency type like entered or total, amount type like period or year to date, scenario and balance type. Most differences end there. Second, timing: were journals posted after the cube last updated, or did an Update Balances Cube process fail? Third, the hierarchy: maybe a tree changed but wasn't republished. Fourth, data access: two users with different access see different numbers. Then I run the trial balance with exactly the same parameters and drill into Account Inspector to find the difference.\"\n\nTip: Start with the point of view. It solves most cases and shows you've done this.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["Which POV setting causes the most mismatches in your experience?","Currency type. Users compare Entered currency in Smart View to a trial balance in Total or ledger currency, especially in multi-currency ledgers. Setting the POV to Total in ledger currency usually reconciles it immediately."]}],
["Fusion GL", "Explain ledger sets and when you'd use them.", "**Why they ask:** ledger sets save a lot of time in multi-entity companies.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A ledger set groups ledgers that share the same chart of accounts and calendar; the currencies can be different. With a ledger set I can open and close periods, run revaluation, translation and allocations, report, and grant data access across many ledgers at once. A typical setup is one set for all European ledgers and a global set for consolidated reporting in GL.\"\n\nTip: Mention the same chart and calendar requirement. It's the rule they check.", "B", null, null, {"f":["Can you put ledgers with different calendars in one ledger set?","No. A ledger set needs the same chart of accounts and accounting calendar. Ledgers with different calendars need separate sets or consolidation in FCCS."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What is a data access set and how does it differ from segment value security?", "**Why they ask:** GL security design affects performance and audit.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A data access set controls which ledgers or ledger sets a user can work in, and which primary balancing segment values, or management segment values, with read-only or read-write access. It drives journal entry, inquiry and period management. Segment value security restricts the values of any segment, like cost center, in entry and reporting. My usual design is data access sets for legal entity scope, and segment security only where it's really needed, because it adds complexity and can slow reports.\"\n\nTip: Recommend data access sets first. Showing restraint with segment security is a senior point.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["Give an example where you'd need segment value security on top of a data access set.","If a cost center manager should only post to their own cost centers within a legal entity they share with others. The data access set grants the ledger and balancing value; segment value security restricts the cost center segment."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How does journal import from external systems work, and how do you make it robust?", "**Why they ask:** integrations into GL fail silently if they aren't designed well.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Journals come in through FBDI with the journal import template, through the ERP Integration Service from OIC, or through REST for small volumes. They load into GL_INTERFACE, then Import Journals runs by ledger, source and group ID, with optional AutoPost. To make it robust I give each system its own journal source, use a group ID per batch, validate account combinations before sending, send control totals, set up alerts on errors, make the load safe against double sending, and run a reconciliation report per source every period.\"\n\nTip: Mention protection against double loads. It's the failure that hurts most.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you prevent duplicate journals when an integration reruns?","I give each load a unique batch name or group ID tied to the source run, and the integration checks whether that batch was already imported before submitting. Reconciling control totals from the source to GL each run also catches duplicates fast."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What is the difference between Account Monitor, Account Inspector and Financial Reports?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you know which tool to give which user.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Account Monitor watches key account balances on the GL dashboard, with thresholds, and lets you drill down. Account Inspector is for ad-hoc analysis: pivot by any segment or hierarchy and drill to journals and subledger detail. Financial Reporting and Smart View are for formatted statements and Excel-based analysis. All three read the same Essbase cube, so they agree with each other.\"\n\nTip: Say they all read the same cube. It shows you understand the architecture.", "B", null, null, {"f":["Which one would you give an FP&A analyst who wants to slice actuals by department and drill to invoices?","Account Inspector for quick exploration, or Smart View ad hoc for Excel-based analysis. Both drill through to journals and subledger detail; Financial Reports are for formatted, repeatable packages."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How do you set up journal approval and what are common issues?", "**Why they ask:** journal approval often slows down the close.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I enable approval at the ledger and journal source level, and the rules live in BPM through Manage Journal Approval Rules, for example by maximum line amount, ledger or category, using the supervisor hierarchy, positions or approval groups. The common problems are missing approvers because a supervisor left, people approving their own journals, subledger sources routed for approval by mistake, and slow approvals during close. The fixes are fallback approvers, delegation, excluding subledger sources, and watching pending approvals every day of close.\"\n\nTip: Give the problems and their fixes as pairs. It sounds like real support experience.", "D", null, null, {"f":["A journal is stuck in approval because the approver left. How do you fix it?","I reassign the task in BPM worklist as an administrator or have the approval rules updated to the new approver. Longer term I use positions or approval groups instead of named people, so departures don't break the routing."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How do you handle a mid-year change to the chart of accounts, such as splitting a cost center?", "**Why they ask:** COA changes touch everything. They want a full checklist.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I create the new values, update the hierarchies and publish the trees. Then I update everything that defaults or uses the old value: HCM assignments, supplier and customer defaults, SLA mapping sets, allocations, cross-validation rules and security. For history, I agree with FP&A whether to reclass existing balances with a journal or keep history on the old value and start fresh. I end-date the old value after a transition period, retest integrations and reports, and tell everyone the effective date.\"\n\nTip: Mention agreeing the history approach with FP&A. It's the decision that causes arguments later.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Would you restate history when you split a cost center?","Usually not in the ledger. I'd keep history on the old cost center and use reporting hierarchies or mappings to show a combined view. If management needs restated history, a reclass journal for current year YTD is the cleanest option."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What GL reports and checks are part of your month-end close?", "**Why they ask:** a practical check of whether you've run a close.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I check unposted journals and journals pending approval first, because nothing else is final until those are done. Then the subledger-to-GL reconciliations for AP, AR, Assets and Inventory, and intercompany reconciliation. Then suspense and clearing account balances, and the revaluation and translation results, including the CTA movement. Then the trial balance by ledger and legal entity, a variance analysis against last period and budget, and a review of manual and top-side journals.\"\n\nTip: Order matters. Starting with unposted and pending journals shows you've run real closes.", "B", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["What's your first check if the AP-to-GL reconciliation doesn't tie?","Unaccounted or unposted transactions: invoices validated but not accounted, or accounting created in final but not transferred to GL. Then manual journals posted directly to the AP liability account, which bypass the subledger."]}],
["Fusion GL", "How would you design the GL for a company reporting under IFRS at group level and US GAAP for a US parent filing?", "**Why they ask:** a senior multi-GAAP design question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For each entity, the primary ledger uses the GAAP with the most transactions or the legal requirement, often local GAAP or IFRS. The other GAAP sits in a secondary ledger: subledger level if many transactions differ, like leases, revenue and assets; adjustment-only if the differences are few. Fixed Assets gets separate corporate books for each GAAP, each linked to its ledger. I'd set up ledger sets per GAAP for close and reporting. Consolidation happens in FCCS, using the Multi-GAAP dimension or separate scenarios for IFRS and US GAAP. And I'd document every GAAP difference and the accounts used for the adjustments.\"\n\nTip: Explain when you'd choose subledger level vs adjustment-only. That choice is the core of the answer.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["When would you use an adjustment-only secondary ledger instead of subledger level?","When the GAAP differences are few and can be posted as manual adjustments each period, like a handful of lease or impairment entries. It's much lighter than subledger level, but the secondary ledger only holds adjustments, so you report by combining it with the primary."]}],
["Fusion GL", "What is an adjusting period and when do you use it?", "**Why they ask:** a basic GL calendar question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An adjusting period is an extra period in the calendar that overlaps the year-end date, like Adj-24 dated December 31. It's used for audit and year-end adjustments without reopening December. The balances roll into the year-end numbers, and retained earnings are recalculated when the new year is opened. The benefit is that December's operating results stay separate from audit adjustments.\"\n\nTip: State the benefit: December stays clean. That's why companies use it.", "B", null, null, {"f":["How many adjusting periods can a calendar have?","You can define more than one, for example an audit adjustments period and a tax adjustments period, all dated on the last day of the year. Each must be part of the calendar before the year is used."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a difficult GL close issue you resolved as a contractor.", "**Why they ask:** a behavioral question to see how you work under close pressure.\n\n**Answer (example, adjust to your own story):**\n\"On day three of a quarter-end close, intercompany was out of balance by $1.2 million across eight entities, and consolidation was due the next day. I ran intercompany reconciliation by trading partner and traced most of it to manual journals with the wrong intercompany segment value, plus some FX timing differences. I corrected the entries with the entity accountants, and we met the deadline. Then I fixed the cause: I added a cross-validation rule so the intercompany segment had to match a valid trading partner, and set up a weekly intercompany matching review. In the following months intercompany differences were close to zero.\"\n\nTip: Use STAR and always end with the permanent fix. Contractors are hired to stop problems coming back.", "B", null, null, {"f":["What would you do differently to stop that intercompany issue recurring?","I'd add intercompany reconciliation checks earlier in the close, on day one rather than day three, and make sure every intercompany journal goes through the balancing rules rather than manual lines. A partner segment on the COA makes matching much faster."]}],
["Behavioral", "As a GL contractor, how do you get productive in the first two weeks?", "**Why they ask:** contractors are expected to deliver fast. They want a plan.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In the first days I read: the enterprise structure, the chart of accounts, the ledger setup, the close calendar, recent audit findings and open tickets. I shadow a month-end close if the timing allows. I map every integration and journal source that feeds GL. I meet the controller, the GL accountants, FP&A, and IT support. By the end of week two I aim for one quick win, like fixing a recurring close problem, and I keep a running log of issues and recommendations that I share with the manager.\"\n\nTip: Mention the quick win. Managers hire contractors who make an early difference.", "B", null, null, {"f":["What do you avoid doing in your first two weeks as a contractor?","Changing setups before I understand why they exist. Many odd configurations have a history, like an audit finding or a downstream report, so I document and ask before I recommend changes."]}]
]);

// Oracle Modules — General Ledger deep dive (part 2: IFRS and multi-GAAP). Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB["ora-mod-gl"] = (QB["ora-mod-gl"] || []).concat([
["IFRS", "What are the key differences between IFRS and US GAAP that affect the General Ledger?", "**Why they ask:** global companies need consultants who can turn GAAP differences into system design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd group the main ones. Inventory: IFRS bans LIFO and allows write-downs to be reversed; US GAAP allows LIFO and no reversal. Fixed assets: IFRS allows the revaluation model and requires component depreciation; US GAAP is cost only. Impairment: IFRS is a one-step test at cash-generating unit level with reversals allowed except goodwill; US GAAP is two-step for long-lived assets with no reversal. Development costs: capitalized under IFRS when criteria are met, expensed under US GAAP except some software. Leases: IFRS 16 has one lessee model; ASC 842 has finance and operating. Provisions: IFRS uses more-likely-than-not and the midpoint of a range; US GAAP has a higher threshold and uses the low end. Credit losses: IFRS 9 three stages; US GAAP CECL lifetime from day one. Consolidation: IFRS 10 single control model; US GAAP voting interest and VIE models. Each one becomes a decision in Oracle: a separate accounting method, a secondary ledger, or an adjustment.\"\n\nTip: End by saying each difference becomes a system decision. That's what makes you the ERP person in the room.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Which of those differences causes the most configuration work in Oracle?","Fixed assets, because IFRS component depreciation and the revaluation model usually mean a separate asset book. Leases are next, since ROU assets and liabilities are measured differently and often need Lease Accounting or extra asset books."]}],
["IFRS", "How do you support IFRS and local GAAP in Oracle Fusion?", "**Why they ask:** a practical design question for global rollouts.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The primary ledger runs in one GAAP and a secondary ledger in the other. For systematic differences, I use subledger level, where SLA creates entries for both GAAPs from the same transaction using different accounting methods. For a few differences, journal or adjustment-only level, where GL journals are copied with conversion rules and GAAP-only adjustments are posted in the secondary ledger. Fixed Assets uses separate corporate books per GAAP, Lease Accounting applies IFRS 16 or ASC 842 per ledger, and a chart of accounts mapping handles a different statutory chart. Reporting runs through ledger sets and FCCS Multi-GAAP.\"\n\nTip: Explain when you use each level. It's the decision they want to hear you make.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you keep the secondary ledger from drifting from the primary?","Reconcile them every period with a GAAP difference report, review replication exceptions, and make sure every new journal source and category has a conversion rule. Any manual entry in one ledger needs a documented reason."]}],
["IFRS", "Explain IFRS 16 lease accounting and how it differs from ASC 842.", "**Why they ask:** leases are a big area where the standards differ, and it affects EBITDA.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under IFRS 16, lessees put almost all leases on the balance sheet as a right-of-use asset and a lease liability, the present value of the payments. The P&L shows depreciation plus interest, which front-loads the expense. There are exemptions for short-term leases of 12 months or less and low-value assets. ASC 842 also puts leases on the balance sheet, but has two models: finance leases, which work like IFRS 16, and operating leases, which show one straight-line lease cost. There's no low-value exemption. In Oracle, Fusion Lease Accounting calculates the ROU asset, liability, schedules and accounting per ledger and GAAP, and payments go to AP. A point I always add: EBITDA is higher under IFRS 16, because lease cost moves below EBITDA into depreciation and interest.\"\n\nTip: The EBITDA point is the one interviewers remember. Don't leave it out.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How does that front-loading show up in the P&L for a five-year lease?","Interest is higher early because the liability is largest at the start, so total expense under IFRS 16 is higher in year one and lower in year five. Under ASC 842 operating leases, expense is straight-line, flat every year."]}],
["IFRS", "Explain IAS 21 foreign currency: functional currency, transactions and translation.", "**Why they ask:** foreign currency drives revaluation and translation setup in GL.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each entity has a functional currency, the currency of its main economic environment, where it sells, buys and borrows. Foreign currency transactions are recorded at the spot rate. Monetary items, like receivables, payables and loans, are retranslated at the closing rate each period, and the differences go to P&L. Non-monetary items stay at the historical rate. To translate into a presentation currency, assets and liabilities use the closing rate, income and expenses use transaction or average rates, and the difference goes to OCI as the translation reserve, which is recycled to P&L if the foreign operation is sold. In Oracle, GL revaluation handles the monetary items, and translation runs in GL with CTA, or in FCCS.\"\n\nTip: Link each part of IAS 21 to the Oracle process. That's what an ERP interviewer wants.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Where do translation differences go under IAS 21?","To other comprehensive income as the foreign currency translation reserve, the IFRS name for CTA. They're reclassified to profit or loss when the foreign operation is disposed of."]}],
["IFRS", "What is IAS 29 hyperinflation and how would you handle it in Oracle?", "**Why they ask:** a specialist question for companies with operations in places like Argentina or Türkiye.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IAS 29 applies when an entity's functional currency is hyperinflationary, with cumulative three-year inflation around 100%, as in Argentina and Türkiye in recent years. The financial statements are restated using a general price index: non-monetary items are indexed, and the gain or loss on the net monetary position goes to P&L. Then the statements are translated at the closing rate, including the P&L. Oracle doesn't do this natively, so it's usually handled with a separate or secondary ledger, or an adjustment process with index-based calculations in spreadsheets or EPM, and translation in FCCS using closing rates for the P&L. I'd also check the hyperinflation list every reporting period, because countries move on and off it.\"\n\nTip: Mention checking the list each period. It shows you've dealt with it, not just read about it.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Can Oracle restate hyperinflation automatically?","Fusion has a hyperinflation option for translation using historical rates, but the index restatement of non-monetary items is often done with adjustment journals from a schedule or in FCCS. I'd confirm what the current release supports before committing."]}],
["IFRS", "What is IFRS 18 and how could it affect ERP and reporting?", "**Why they ask:** IFRS 18 is coming soon, and they want to know you're ahead of it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IFRS 18, Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements, replaces IAS 1 for annual periods starting on or after 1 January 2027, with comparatives restated. It changes the P&L structure: income and expenses are classified as operating, investing or financing, plus income taxes and discontinued operations, with required subtotals for operating profit and profit before financing and income taxes. Management-defined performance measures must be disclosed and reconciled in the notes, and there's more guidance on aggregation. For the ERP, that means classifying accounts into the new categories through COA attributes or hierarchies, reviewing where FX and interest are classified, redesigning report templates in Financial Reporting, FCCS or Narrative Reporting, and restating the mapping for comparatives.\"\n\nTip: Mention the comparatives. Restating the prior year is the work that companies underestimate.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What should a finance team do now to prepare for IFRS 18?","Map every P&L account to the new operating, investing and financing categories, identify management-defined performance measures, and plan for restated comparatives. In Oracle that usually means new account attributes or hierarchies and updated financial reports."]}],
["IFRS", "How are provisions and contingent liabilities treated under IAS 37?", "**Why they ask:** provisions are judgment-heavy, and GL teams book and track them.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A provision is recognized when there's a present obligation from a past event, an outflow is probable, meaning more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated reliably. It's measured at the best estimate: expected value for large populations, the most likely outcome for a single obligation, or the midpoint when all points in a range are equally likely, and discounted if material. Contingent liabilities are disclosed but not recognized, unless remote, in which case nothing is disclosed. Contingent assets are disclosed when probable and recognized only when virtually certain. Onerous contracts and restructuring have their own rules. In GL that means dedicated provision accounts, a monthly review and true-up, and documentation for audit.\"\n\nTip: Give the measurement options clearly. It's where IFRS differs from US GAAP.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How would you record a provision in Oracle?","Through a manual or recurring journal to an expense and a provision liability account, with support for the estimate. If it's discounted, the unwinding goes to finance cost each period, which I'd automate with a recurring journal."]}],
["IFRS", "Explain IAS 36 impairment testing.", "**Why they ask:** impairment is a big judgment area, and finance systems need to support it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At each reporting date you look for impairment indicators, and goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles are tested at least once a year. The recoverable amount is the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, which is discounted cash flows. Testing is done at the cash-generating unit level, and goodwill is allocated to CGUs. The loss is the carrying amount minus the recoverable amount, allocated first to goodwill, then to the other assets pro rata. Reversals are allowed, except for goodwill, up to what the carrying amount would have been without the impairment. In Oracle, I use the Assets impairment functionality or journals, and FCCS for goodwill at group level.\"\n\nTip: Mention the order of allocation: goodwill first. It's the detail that shows real understanding.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Can you reverse an impairment under IFRS?","Yes for most assets, up to what the carrying amount would have been without the impairment, but never for goodwill. US GAAP doesn't allow reversals for assets held and used."]}],
["IFRS", "What is component depreciation under IAS 16 and how do you set it up in Oracle Assets?", "**Why they ask:** component depreciation is an IFRS requirement that changes asset design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under IAS 16, significant parts of an asset with different useful lives are depreciated separately, for example an aircraft's body, engines and interior, or a building's structure, roof and HVAC. In Oracle Assets I set each component up as its own asset, linked through a parent-child hierarchy, with its own category and life. If the US GAAP book keeps it as one asset, I use separate corporate books for each GAAP, so each book holds the structure its standard needs.\"\n\nTip: Give the building example with lives, like 40 years for the structure and 15 for HVAC.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you handle replacing a component, like a roof?","I retire the old roof asset, including its remaining net book value, and add the new roof as a new component asset. That keeps the building's cost accurate and avoids depreciating something that no longer exists."]}],
["IFRS", "How does the IAS 16 revaluation model work and how would you account for it?", "**Why they ask:** revaluation is only allowed under IFRS, so it's a test of global experience.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A class of property, plant and equipment is carried at fair value on the revaluation date, less later depreciation and impairment, and revaluations must be regular enough that the carrying amount stays close to fair value. An increase goes to OCI as revaluation surplus, unless it reverses an earlier decrease that went to P&L. A decrease goes to P&L, unless it reverses an earlier surplus, in which case it reduces OCI. The surplus can be moved to retained earnings as the asset is used or sold. Oracle Assets supports revaluation, with options to revalue accumulated depreciation and to amortize the reserve.\"\n\nTip: Explain the \"unless it reverses\" rule both ways. It's what they're listening for.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What happens if a revalued asset later falls in value?","The decrease first reduces the revaluation surplus in OCI for that asset; anything beyond it goes to profit or loss. In Oracle I record it in the IFRS book with a revaluation journal."]}],
["IFRS", "Explain IFRS 9 expected credit loss for receivables.", "**Why they ask:** the bad debt allowance is a monthly GL entry, and IFRS 9 changed how it's calculated.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IFRS 9 replaced the incurred loss model with expected credit loss, so you recognize expected losses from the start. For trade receivables and contract assets, the simplified approach uses lifetime ECL, usually with a provision matrix: loss rates by ageing bucket, adjusted for forward-looking information. For loans there's the general approach in three stages: 12-month ECL in stage 1, lifetime ECL in stage 2 after a significant increase in credit risk, and stage 3 for credit-impaired assets. In Oracle, the AR aging feeds an allowance calculation, often in a spreadsheet or EPM, which is posted to GL, with the method documented. Under US GAAP, CECL requires lifetime losses from day one for everything in scope.\"\n\nTip: Describe the provision matrix with an example, like 1% current, 5% over 30 days, 50% over 90.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you build a provision matrix?","I calculate historical loss rates by ageing bucket, say 0.5% for current up to 40% for over 120 days, adjust them for forward-looking factors like economic outlook, and apply them to the current ageing. The result is the allowance, booked through a GL journal."]}],
["IFRS", "What does IFRS 10 say about control and consolidation?", "**Why they ask:** consolidation scope drives the entity and ledger design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under IFRS 10, an investor controls an investee when it has power over the relevant activities, exposure to variable returns, and the ability to use its power to affect those returns. Control, not just more than 50% of votes, decides consolidation, so it includes de facto control, potential voting rights and structured entities. Non-controlling interests are shown in equity. Changes in ownership that keep control are equity transactions; losing control means remeasuring any retained interest and taking a gain or loss to P&L. Associates under IAS 28 and joint ventures under IFRS 11 use the equity method, while joint operations record their share of assets and liabilities.\"\n\nTip: Name the three elements of control first. It's the core of the standard.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Give an example of control without a majority of votes.","An investor with 45% where the rest is widely spread among hundreds of small holders who don't coordinate, so it can direct the relevant activities in practice. Contractual rights, like appointing most of the board, can also give control."]}],
["IFRS", "How does IAS 12 deferred tax work and how do you support it from the GL?", "**Why they ask:** the tax provision needs good data from the ERP.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Deferred tax arises from temporary differences between an item's carrying amount and its tax base, like depreciation differences, provisions and leases. Deferred tax assets are recognized to the extent future taxable profit is probable; deferred tax liabilities are generally recognized. Rates used are the ones enacted or substantively enacted. From the GL side, I support it with tax books against corporate books in Assets, account attributes that flag tax-relevant accounts, and reports by entity. The calculation is usually done in Tax Reporting in EPM or a tax provision tool and booked back as journals. Pillar Two, the global minimum tax, now adds more data needs and IAS 12 disclosures.\"\n\nTip: Mention Pillar Two. It's current and shows you follow changes.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What GL setup helps the tax team with deferred tax?","Separate accounts for items with known temporary differences, like depreciation by book and tax, and a tax book in Assets. Clean account detail lets the tax team build the temporary difference schedule without manual analysis."]}],
["IFRS", "What are the main IFRS vs US GAAP differences in cash flow statements?", "**Why they ask:** cash flow classification affects account mapping and reporting.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under the current IAS 7, companies choose where to classify interest and dividends paid and received: operating, investing or financing. The IFRS 18 amendments to IAS 7, from 2027, remove most of that choice: for most entities interest paid goes to financing, interest and dividends received go to investing, and the indirect method starts from operating profit. Under US GAAP, interest paid and received and dividends received are operating, and dividends paid are financing. For the ERP, that means account mapping or hierarchies for the cash flow, movement accounts, or the Movement dimension in FCCS.\"\n\nTip: Mention the 2027 change. It shows you're current.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Where will interest paid go for most companies after IFRS 18?","To financing activities, and interest and dividends received to investing, for most non-financial companies. That removes the choice that made IFRS cash flow statements hard to compare."]}],
["IFRS", "What is IFRS 1 and when does it matter in an ERP implementation?", "**Why they ask:** a company moving to IFRS often does it with an ERP project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IFRS 1 covers first-time adoption. The company prepares an opening IFRS balance sheet at the transition date and applies IFRS backwards, with some optional exemptions, like deemed cost for property, plant and equipment, resetting cumulative translation differences to zero, and not restating past business combinations, plus some mandatory exceptions. For the ERP, it means converting balances to IFRS at the transition date in a secondary or adjustment ledger, handling comparative periods, mapping accounts, and documenting every exemption the company chose.\"\n\nTip: Name one or two exemptions. It shows you know the standard, not just its name.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What's the most common IFRS 1 exemption you've seen used?","Deemed cost for property, plant and equipment, using fair value at transition as the new cost. It saves rebuilding years of asset history, which matters a lot when migrating to a new ERP at the same time."]}],
["IFRS", "How are borrowing costs and investment property treated under IFRS?", "**Why they ask:** two IFRS areas that change how assets are accounted for in the ERP.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under IAS 23, borrowing costs directly tied to a qualifying asset, one that takes a long time to get ready, like a plant or a building under construction, must be capitalized. In Oracle I'd capitalize that interest into CIP, either through Projects or by journal. Under IAS 40, investment property, like a building held for rent, can use the fair value model, where changes go to P&L and there's no depreciation, or the cost model with fair value disclosed. US GAAP has no separate investment property model; it stays at cost, except for investment companies.\"\n\nTip: Give an example of a qualifying asset. It makes the rule easy to picture.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you calculate capitalized interest if the project uses general borrowings?","Apply a capitalization rate, the weighted average cost of the general borrowings, to the expenditure on the asset. For example, $10 million average spend at 6% capitalizes $600,000 for the year, capped at actual interest incurred."]}],
["IFRS", "What is IFRS 15 and how do you support it in Oracle?", "**Why they ask:** revenue recognition is high-risk, and they want to know you can link the standard to the system.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IFRS 15 has five steps: identify the contract, identify the performance obligations, set the transaction price including variable consideration, allocate it by relative standalone selling price, and recognize revenue when or as each obligation is satisfied. It's largely the same as ASC 606, with small differences in some practical expedients, licenses and onerous contracts, which IFRS handles under IAS 37. In Oracle, Revenue Management handles contracts, obligations, SSP, allocation and recognition, integrated with Order Management, AR and Projects, and SLA sends the accounting to GL, including contract assets and liabilities.\"\n\nTip: Map each of the five steps to what Revenue Management does. It's the strongest version of this answer.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Which Oracle product supports IFRS 15 allocation?","Revenue Management, which identifies performance obligations, allocates by standalone selling price and recognizes revenue as obligations are satisfied, creating accounting through SLA to GL."]}],
["IFRS", "What disclosures does IFRS 8 operating segments require, and what does it mean for the COA?", "**Why they ask:** segment reporting is designed into the chart of accounts.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IFRS 8 uses the management approach: segments are what the chief operating decision maker reviews. The company discloses segment profit or loss, segment assets if they're reviewed, reconciliations to the totals, and entity-wide information on products, geographies and major customers. For the ERP, that means a segment in the chart of accounts, like business unit or product line, and if balance sheet by segment is needed, balancing by that segment too. The hierarchies must match management reporting so the segment numbers come straight from the ledger.\"\n\nTip: Say \"design the segment into the COA from day one\". Adding it later is expensive.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you make segment reporting easy from the GL?","Make sure the segment management reviews is a COA segment or a clean hierarchy, like product line or region. Then segment profit and assets come straight out of Smart View instead of spreadsheets."]}],
["IFRS", "How do you keep IFRS and US GAAP books reconciled?", "**Why they ask:** multi-GAAP only works if differences are tracked and explained.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep a GAAP difference register that lists each difference, its accounts, amounts and owner. Each period I produce an equity and net income bridge between the two ledgers or scenarios. Adjustment-only entries need approval and documentation. Where a difference repeats every month, I automate it, through SLA rules, separate asset books or Lease Accounting, so it's not a manual journal. In FCCS, the Multi-GAAP dimension shows the adjustments separately and totals both views.\"\n\nTip: Mention the difference register. It's the document auditors ask for first.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Who signs off the GAAP bridge each period?","The controller or technical accounting lead, with each reconciling item owned by a named person. The bridge is retained as audit evidence with the supporting calculations."]}],
["IFRS", "A client moving from US GAAP to IFRS asks what changes in their ERP. What do you tell them?", "**Why they ask:** a consulting question. They want you to lead the client through it in order.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd tell them the policy decisions come first, before any configuration: the PP&E model, component depreciation, development costs, leases, provisions, expected credit loss, and inventory costing without LIFO. Then the ledger strategy, primary and secondary, and the asset book design. Then SLA rule changes for the treatments that differ. Then COA and hierarchies for the new presentation, including IFRS 18 from 2027. Then the opening balance conversion under IFRS 1 and the comparatives. And last, reporting templates, FCCS rules, and training for the accountants.\"\n\nTip: Start with \"policy decisions first\". It's what separates a consultant from a configurator.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What's the first thing you'd do in week one of that project?","Run a policy workshop with the controller and auditors to list the IFRS accounting choices, because every configuration decision depends on them. Then I'd draft the ledger strategy for review."]}],
["IFRS", "Which IFRS standards have changed recently or are coming that a GL consultant should watch?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you keep up to date.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The big one is IFRS 18, presentation and disclosure, effective 1 January 2027 with restated comparatives. IFRS 19, reduced disclosures for subsidiaries without public accountability, is also effective in 2027. The IAS 12 Pillar Two amendments added a temporary exception for deferred taxes on the global minimum tax, plus disclosures. The IAS 21 amendments on lack of exchangeability took effect in 2025. And the IFRS 9 and IFRS 7 amendments on classification and measurement apply from 2026. I follow the IASB work plan and the client's auditor updates to stay current.\"\n\nTip: Say how you stay current. It matters as much as the list.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What is the Pillar Two exception in IAS 12?","A mandatory temporary exception from recognizing deferred taxes related to OECD Pillar Two global minimum tax, with extra disclosures. It matters for groups with revenue over 750 million euros."]}]
]);

// Oracle Modules — General Ledger deep dive (part 3: FCCS consolidation and close). Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB["ora-mod-gl"] = (QB["ora-mod-gl"] || []).concat([
["FCCS", "What is Oracle FCCS and how does it fit with Fusion GL?", "**Why they ask:** many GL roles now include consolidation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"FCCS, Financial Consolidation and Close, is Oracle's cloud EPM application for group consolidation and close. It handles currency translation, intercompany eliminations, ownership and non-controlling interest, consolidation journals, the cash flow statement and close task management. Fusion GL stays the book of record for each ledger. FCCS receives trial balances by entity, account, period and currency, often with intercompany partner and movement, through Data Integration, with drill-through back to Fusion. Reporting is in Financial Reports, Smart View and Narrative Reporting.\"\n\nTip: Say \"GL is the book of record\". It shows you understand who owns what.", "B", null, null, {"f":["Would you recommend FCCS for a company with three entities in one currency?","Probably not. Fusion GL ledger sets, intercompany and Financial Reporting can handle that. FCCS earns its cost with many entities, currencies, partial ownership or complex close requirements."]}],
["FCCS", "What are the standard dimensions in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** dimension design is the core of any FCCS project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The standard dimensions are Account, Entity, Period, Years, Scenario, View, Currency, Consolidation, Intercompany, Data Source and Movement. There's an optional Multi-GAAP dimension and a small number of custom dimensions, like Product or Cost Center, which have to be chosen carefully because there's a limit and they affect performance. The Consolidation dimension tracks the stages, entity input, proportion, elimination and contribution, and Data Source tracks where the data came from, like a data load, a journal, supplemental data or a calculation.\"\n\nTip: Mention the limit on custom dimensions. It's the design decision that matters most.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Why limit custom dimensions in FCCS?","Every dimension multiplies the data and slows consolidation. Oracle limits custom dimensions, so I only add one when management truly needs to report consolidated results by it, like product."]}],
["FCCS", "Walk me through the FCCS consolidation process.", "**Why they ask:** they want to see you understand the whole flow, not just individual features.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It starts with loading trial balances and supplemental data for each entity. Then entity-level calculations run. Then translation into the parent's currency, using average and ending rates with historical overrides, and FX and CTA calculated through the Movement dimension. Then proportionalization by ownership and consolidation method. Then eliminations of intercompany balances and investments at the first common parent. Then each entity contributes its results to the parent, and the process repeats up the hierarchy. Top-side adjustments go in as journals, approvals move through the approval unit hierarchy, and before anyone reports I check the calculation status, OK, Impacted or System Changed.\"\n\nTip: End with the calculation status check. It's the step that prevents wrong numbers in reports.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["What would you check if consolidation runs but totals look wrong?","Calculation status first, then the translation rates and historical overrides, then intercompany eliminations and ownership settings. Comparing Entity Input to the GL trial balance tells me whether it's a load issue or a consolidation logic issue."]}],
["FCCS", "How does intercompany elimination work in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** intercompany is the most common source of consolidation problems.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Intercompany balances are loaded with the Intercompany dimension filled in, which comes from the GL intercompany segment. Intercompany matching reports compare receivables to payables, and revenue to expense, between each pair of partners, with tolerances. During consolidation, the balances are eliminated at the first common parent, and any difference goes to a plug account. When pairs don't match, the usual causes are timing, different FX rates, a missing partner segment, or a posting to the wrong account, and I fix those in GL where possible rather than with a top-side journal.\"\n\nTip: Say \"fix it at the source in GL\". Auditors prefer it, and so do controllers.", "D", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["What happens to an intercompany difference that doesn't match?","It goes to a plug account so the consolidated balance sheet still balances, and the difference report shows which partner pair caused it. The fix belongs in the source ledgers, not in FCCS."]}],
["FCCS", "How does FCCS handle currency translation and CTA?", "**Why they ask:** translation is where consolidation numbers get questioned.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Exchange rates are entered by period, average and ending, and historical rates or amounts are set as overrides for equity and investments. The P&L is translated at average rates, the balance sheet at ending rates, and equity at historical rates. FCCS calculates FX on opening balances and FX on movements in the Movement dimension, and the difference flows to CTA in OCI. The Currency dimension shows entity currency, parent currency and adjustments. It can also be configured for hyperinflation translation methods.\"\n\nTip: Mention FX on opening vs FX on movement. It shows you understand how CTA is built.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you handle historical rates for equity in the first year of FCCS?","I load historical translated amounts for equity and investment accounts as overrides at go-live, based on the prior system. After that, new equity transactions get their own historical rates."]}],
["FCCS", "How does ownership management and NCI work in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** partial ownership is where consolidation gets complex.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each entity has ownership settings under its parent: ownership percentage, control percentage, and a consolidation method, like holding, subsidiary for full consolidation, proportional, equity or discontinued. For partially owned subsidiaries, FCCS calculates non-controlling interest and performs the investment and equity eliminations. Ownership can change by period for acquisitions and disposals, and configurable consolidation rules handle special cases.\"\n\nTip: Give a simple example, like 80% owned means 20% NCI in equity and profit.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How is NCI calculated for an 80%-owned subsidiary with $1 million profit?","FCCS allocates 20%, $200,000, of the subsidiary's profit and equity to non-controlling interest, shown separately in equity and in the P&L attribution. The parent's share is $800,000."]}],
["FCCS", "How do you integrate Fusion GL with FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** a practical integration question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use Data Integration with a Fusion GL source connection, choosing the ledgers, balance types and periods. Then the mappings: GL account to FCCS account, balancing segment or legal entity to Entity, intercompany segment to Intercompany, currency, and optionally cost center or product to custom dimensions. Period and category mappings follow, and load rules run per period on a schedule during close. Validation checks catch unmapped members, and drill-through goes back to Fusion GL balances and journals. Writing FCCS journals back to GL is possible but usually not needed.\"\n\nTip: Name the key mapping, intercompany segment to the Intercompany dimension. It's what makes eliminations work.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How often do you load GL data to FCCS during close?","Usually several times: a first load after the soft close, then incremental reloads as late adjustments post. The integration is scheduled, and the final load happens when GL is closed."]}],
["FCCS", "What is the Movement dimension and why is it useful?", "**Why they ask:** movement data drives the cash flow statement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The Movement dimension holds the roll-forward of each balance sheet account: opening balance, increases, decreases, acquisitions, disposals, FX on opening, FX on movement, and closing. It drives the automated cash flow statement using the indirect method, and roll-forward disclosures like PP&E and equity. To work, it needs movement detail from the GL through mappings or supplemental data, or calculated movements.\"\n\nTip: Say it drives the cash flow. That's why it matters to the business.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What happens if subledgers don't capture movement detail?","The cash flow statement relies on defaults, putting most of the change into one movement, which needs manual reclassification. I map GL accounts and journal categories to movement members where possible, or collect detail through Supplemental Data Manager."]}],
["FCCS", "What is the Multi-GAAP dimension used for?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you know how FCCS supports more than one accounting standard.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It stores local GAAP, the IFRS or US GAAP adjustments, and the total for the other GAAP in the same application. For example, I load local GAAP trial balances, enter or calculate the IFRS adjustments, and report IFRS consolidated results. The other option is separate scenarios, but Multi-GAAP keeps the adjustments visible and easy to reconcile.\"\n\nTip: Give the local GAAP to IFRS example. It's the common case.", "D", null, null, {"f":["When would you use a separate scenario instead of Multi-GAAP?","When the groups reporting under each GAAP have different processes and timelines, or the adjustments are large and complex. Multi-GAAP is simpler for a few adjustments reported together."]}],
["FCCS", "How do journals work in FCCS and when would you use them?", "**Why they ask:** too many top-side journals is a warning sign in any close.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"FCCS journals are for top-side adjustments: reclassifications, consolidation entries, IFRS adjustments, and late adjustments that shouldn't go back to the local ledger. They have workflow, submit, approve, post, and can be recurring or auto-reversing, posted to specific Consolidation and Data Source members. My rule is to keep top-side journals to a minimum and correct at source in GL wherever possible, because that keeps the audit trail clean.\"\n\nTip: Say you minimize top-side journals. It's a sign of a well-run close.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Why not post every adjustment back in the local ledger?","Consolidation-only entries, like eliminations of investments or top-side reclasses, don't belong in any single entity's books. Local ledgers should hold local truth; FCCS journals hold group-level adjustments with their own approval trail."]}],
["FCCS", "What is Task Manager / Close Manager in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** close management is part of most FCCS projects.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Task Manager runs the close calendar: tasks, owners, dependencies, due dates, integration tasks like a data load, approvals and evidence. Dashboards show status across all entities, so the controller sees what's late. It helps shorten and control the close, and it's often used with Account Reconciliation for balance sheet reconciliations.\"\n\nTip: Mention dependencies. They're what make it more than a checklist.", "B", null, null, {"f":["How do you measure whether Task Manager actually shortened the close?","Track days to close and the number of late tasks period over period. When a task is consistently late, the dashboard shows where to fix the process."]}],
["FCCS", "What is Supplemental Data Manager and Enterprise Journals?", "**Why they ask:** these tools fill the gaps between GL and consolidation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Supplemental Data Manager collects detail that isn't in the GL, like debt schedules, lease details, intercompany transaction detail and disclosure data, through forms with workflow, and it can feed consolidation accounts. Enterprise Journals manages journals with workflow and posts them to the ERP or to FCCS, which is useful for controlled top-side and recurring entries.\"\n\nTip: Give one example for each. Debt schedules for supplemental data is easy to remember.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Give an example of data you'd collect in Supplemental Data Manager.","Lease schedules for disclosures, debt maturity tables, or intercompany transaction detail by invoice for matching. Anything the notes need that isn't in the trial balance."]}],
["FCCS", "What does the approval unit hierarchy do?", "**Why they ask:** close controls depend on it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It defines who prepares, reviews and approves each entity's data, by entity, scenario and period, and data moves up through the levels as it's promoted. Once approved, the data is locked, so nobody can change it after sign-off. It gives a controlled and auditable close.\"\n\nTip: Mention the lock after approval. That's the control auditors care about.", "B", null, null, {"f":["What happens if an approved entity needs a late change?","An administrator or the approver has to reject or unlock the approval unit, the change is made and re-promoted. That leaves an audit trail of who reopened it and why."]}],
["FCCS", "What is the calculation status and why do you care?", "**Why they ask:** a classic FCCS trick question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each entity and period has a status: OK means consolidated and current; Impacted means data changed since the last consolidation; System Changed means metadata or rates changed; No Data means nothing's there. Before anyone reports, every entity should be OK, and if not, I rerun consolidation. If someone says two reports show different numbers, the first thing I check is the calculation status and the rates.\"\n\nTip: Give the two-reports-differ scenario. It's often the exact interview question.", "B", null, null, {"f":["An entity shows Impacted right before the board pack. What do you do?","Rerun consolidation for that entity and its parents, then confirm the numbers didn't change materially from what was reviewed. If they did, I'd find the late change before releasing anything."]}],
["FCCS", "How would you design the entity and account hierarchies in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** hierarchies decide how consolidation and reporting work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The entity hierarchy follows legal ownership, because that drives consolidation and NCI, with alternate hierarchies for management views or regions using shared members. The account hierarchy follows the financial statements and includes the system accounts FCCS needs, like total balance sheet and income statement, plus rate and ownership accounts. Mappings from the GL chart of accounts have clear owners. And I keep custom dimensions to a minimum for performance.\"\n\nTip: Say the entity hierarchy follows legal ownership. It's the rule that makes NCI correct.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Why use shared members for management hierarchies?","They let an entity appear in a regional rollup without changing its legal parent, so consolidation and ownership stay correct while management gets its own view."]}],
["FCCS", "How does FCCS produce the cash flow statement?", "**Why they ask:** automated cash flow is one of the main reasons companies buy FCCS.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It uses the Movement dimension. For each balance sheet account, movements are tagged, like increases, decreases, acquisitions, non-cash and FX. The cash flow accounts are calculated from those movements using the indirect method, with the FX effect on cash shown separately. It only works as well as the movement data, so I make sure the GL mappings or supplemental data provide it.\"\n\nTip: Say it depends on movement data quality. That's the honest part interviewers respect.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How is the FX effect on cash calculated?","From the FX on opening and FX on movement members of the cash accounts, which capture translation changes. It appears as a separate line so operating, investing and financing flows aren't distorted by currency moves."]}],
["FCCS", "What are common FCCS implementation challenges?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you've lived through an FCCS project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The common ones are mapping quality from the GL, especially accounts, entities and intercompany partner; intercompany discipline in the source ledgers; getting movement data for the cash flow; ownership complexity and historical rates for equity; performance when there are too many custom dimensions; and change management for finance users moving from spreadsheets or HFM. In my experience, intercompany discipline is the one that decides whether the project succeeds.\"\n\nTip: Pick one as the biggest and say why. It shows judgment, not just a list.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Which of those challenges do you tackle first?","Mapping and intercompany discipline in the source ledgers, because every downstream problem traces back to them. A clean partner segment and consistent account mapping fix most consolidation surprises."]}],
["FCCS", "How does FCCS compare to Hyperion Financial Management (HFM)?", "**Why they ask:** many FCCS projects are HFM migrations.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"FCCS is the cloud successor to HFM. It comes with built-in consolidation logic, dimensions and reports, and Oracle updates it every quarter. It needs much less custom rules coding, using configurable calculations instead of HFM rules files. It has close management, supplemental data and integrations built in. A migration means redesigning the dimensions and rules, not copying HFM as it is.\"\n\nTip: Say \"not a lift and shift\". It's the key message for an HFM client.", "D", null, null, {"f":["What's the biggest risk when migrating from HFM to FCCS?","Trying to recreate every custom HFM rule. Many can be replaced by FCCS built-in logic or configurable calculations, so I review each rule and keep only the ones with a real requirement."]}],
["FCCS", "What is the difference between consolidating in Fusion GL and in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you can recommend the right tool.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Fusion GL can consolidate with ledger sets, translation, intercompany balancing and elimination journals, which is fine for simpler groups. FCCS is a full consolidation engine: ownership and NCI, equity eliminations, multi-GAAP, movement-based cash flow, journals with workflow and close tasks. My rule of thumb: many entities, partial ownership, or statutory consolidated reporting means FCCS.\"\n\nTip: Give the rule of thumb. It answers \"which would you choose?\" before they ask.", "B", null, null, {"f":["At what point would you move from GL consolidation to FCCS?","When there's partial ownership, many currencies, multi-GAAP needs, or the close depends on spreadsheets for eliminations and cash flow. Those are signs GL alone isn't enough."]}],
["FCCS", "How do you reconcile FCCS to Fusion GL?", "**Why they ask:** if FCCS doesn't tie to GL, nobody trusts it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I run the data load validation reports, totals by entity and account against the GL trial balance, and drill through to GL for any difference. Then I check mapping changes, unmapped members, and period and category mappings. Then top-side journals in FCCS, which the Data Source dimension shows separately. And each entity signs off through the approval process.\"\n\nTip: Mention the Data Source dimension. It's the fastest way to see what changed after the load.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you stop unmapped members from slipping through?","I set Data Integration to fail or flag on unmapped values and review the validation report every load. New GL values must be added to the FCCS mapping as part of the GL change process."]}],
["FCCS", "Explain the Consolidation dimension members.", "**Why they ask:** understanding these is how you explain parent numbers to finance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Entity Input is the data loaded or entered for the entity. Entity Consolidation, or Entity Total, is that data after entity-level calculations and adjustments. Proportion is the entity's data multiplied by the ownership percentage. Elimination holds intercompany and investment eliminations. Contribution is what the entity adds to its parent: proportion plus eliminations plus contribution adjustments. When someone asks why the parent isn't the sum of its children, the answer is in these members.\"\n\nTip: End with the parent-vs-children question. It's the real use of this knowledge.", "D", null, null, {"f":["Where do you see the NCI share in the Consolidation dimension?","In the Proportion and Contribution members: Proportion applies the ownership percentage, and the remaining share flows to NCI through the consolidation rules."]}],
["FCCS", "How do you handle an acquisition mid-year in FCCS?", "**Why they ask:** acquisitions test whether you can handle real consolidation events.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I add the new entity to the hierarchy with ownership effective from the acquisition period. I load its opening balance sheet at acquisition, with fair values and goodwill through journals or supplemental data. Acquisition movements are recorded for the balance sheet and cash flow. If ownership is partial, NCI is calculated. Translation and consolidation start from the acquisition date, and prior periods stay unchanged.\"\n\nTip: Mention acquisition movements in the cash flow. It's the detail most people miss.", "D", null, null, {"f":["How do you treat the acquired company's profit before the acquisition date?","It's excluded from group results. Only profit after acquisition is consolidated; the pre-acquisition equity is eliminated against the investment along with goodwill."]}],
["FCCS", "How do Fusion GL and FCCS work together during month-end close?", "**Why they ask:** they want the full close sequence across both systems.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In Fusion, subledgers close, then GL adjustments, revaluation and intercompany, and GL is closed or soft-closed. Then a scheduled data load sends the trial balances to FCCS. Entities review their data, translation and consolidation run, top-side journals are posted, approvals move up, and reporting follows. Task Manager orchestrates both systems, and Account Reconciliation runs in parallel. If a late GL adjustment comes in, we reload and reconsolidate, and the calculation status shows which entities are impacted.\"\n\nTip: Mention late adjustments and calc status. It's the real-world part.", "B", null, null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the cut-off rule for late GL adjustments once FCCS has loaded?","Usually a defined deadline after which material adjustments go to FCCS as top-side journals, with the local ledger corrected next period. Smaller items wait for next month."]}],
["Interview Prep", "Give a 60-second summary of your GL, IFRS and FCCS experience for this interview.", "**Why they ask:** they want a clear, confident summary to anchor the rest of the interview.\n\n**Answer (sample, replace the facts with yours):**\n\"I'm an Oracle Financials consultant with ten years in EBS and Fusion GL and subledgers. I've designed multi-ledger structures with primary, secondary and reporting currency ledgers, for example for a global manufacturer with twelve ledgers and USD reporting currencies for the group. I also delivered IFRS reporting for a US company with European subsidiaries, using secondary ledgers so the European entities could report under IFRS while the group reported under US GAAP. I know the IFRS and US GAAP differences that drive ledger and SLA design, and I know the close end to end: revaluation, translation, intercompany, allocations and consolidation in FCCS. As a contractor, I learn the close quickly and fix what slows it down.\"\n\nTip: Practice it out loud until it takes under a minute. Every fact should be one you can expand on.", "B", null, null, {"f":["If they ask you to go deeper on one project from that summary, which would you choose?","The one with the most complexity and a measurable outcome, like a multi-ledger IFRS design that cut close by two days. I'd be ready to explain the decisions, the problems and the result in about two minutes."]}],
["Interview Prep", "What questions should you ask the client at the end of a GL/IFRS/FCCS interview?", "**Why they ask:** good questions show you're already thinking like part of their team.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd ask: How many ledgers and entities do you have, and which GAAPs do you report under? Is consolidation in FCCS today, or are you moving from HFM or spreadsheets? What are the biggest close pain points, like days to close, intercompany or top-side journals? Is IFRS 18 or any other standard change in scope? And what would success look like in the first 90 days of this contract?\"\n\nTip: Always ask the 90-day question. It tells you what they'll judge you on.", "B", null, null, {"f":["Why ask about close pain points specifically?","It shows I'm thinking about their business problems, not just the system, and their answer tells me where my experience will matter most in the role."]}]
]);

QB['ora-mod-gl'] = (QB['ora-mod-gl'] || []).concat([["Enterprise Structures","Design the enterprise structure for a company with 40 legal entities in 15 countries, a US parent reporting in USD under US GAAP.","**Why they ask:** a senior architecture question. They want clear choices and trade-offs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For ledgers, I'd create primary ledgers per country and currency, using one global chart of accounts where possible. For each non-USD ledger, I'd add a USD reporting currency if the business needs daily USD visibility; otherwise translation in consolidation is enough. Secondary ledgers only where local GAAP differs materially and must be kept in Oracle; otherwise local adjustments go in a statutory tool. Ledger sets by region make period management easier. Legal entities get balancing segment values; business units go by country or region, with shared services BUs providing AP and AR. FCCS handles group consolidation, eliminations and minority interests. The trade-offs I'd walk the client through are a global vs local chart, the number of ledgers vs close effort, and the translation approach.\"\n\nTip: State the trade-offs at the end. Architects are judged on how they explain choices.","D"],["Enterprise Structures","When would you put multiple legal entities in one ledger and when would you separate them?","**Why they ask:** a key design decision in every Fusion project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Entities can share a ledger when they share the four Cs: chart of accounts, calendar, currency and accounting method. Then each entity gets its own balancing segment values. That means fewer ledgers to close, simpler intercompany and shared reporting. I'd separate them when any of the four Cs differ, or when statutory reporting or local auditors need strict separation, when close timelines differ, when security is easier at ledger level, or when a divestiture is planned and a clean carve-out matters. The trade-off with many entities in one ledger is that intercompany balancing rules and security by balancing segment need careful design.\"\n\nTip: Mention the divestiture case. It shows you think beyond the setup screen.","D"],["EPM","How do Oracle EPM modules fit with Fusion Financials?","**Why they ask:** finance architects need to see the whole Oracle finance stack.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Planning handles budgets and forecasts, and approved budgets can go to GL for budgetary control or reporting. FCCS handles consolidation and close. Account Reconciliation handles balance sheet reconciliations and transaction matching. Narrative Reporting builds management and board packages. Tax Reporting does the income tax provision. Profitability and Cost Management handles allocations and profitability. They connect to Fusion through Data Integration, sending GL balances to EPM with drill-back to Fusion. The key is designing accounts and entities consistently across GL and EPM.\"\n\nTip: End with consistent dimensions. It's the architect's real job here.","D"],["Chart of Accounts","A client wants to add a new segment to the COA two years after go-live. How do you respond?","**Why they ask:** a real consulting situation. They want you to push back with options, not just say no.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I'd ask what reporting is missing, because often it can be done without a new segment: hierarchies, supporting references, descriptive flexfields, projects or an EPM dimension. If a segment really is needed, Fusion doesn't let you just add a segment to a chart that's in use. The options are a new chart and new ledgers with a COA mapping and balance migration, or using a spare future segment if one was designed in. The impact is wide: integrations, SLA rules, reports, EPM, security, cross-validation rules and training. So I'd recommend a business case and doing it at a fiscal year boundary. And the lesson for every new design is to include a future segment.\"\n\nTip: Lead with \"what reporting is missing?\" It shows you solve the need, not the request.","D"],["Chart of Accounts","What makes a chart of accounts \"future-proof\"?","**Why they ask:** COA design is a long-term decision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each segment has one clear purpose, no overloaded segments. Value ranges have room to grow, and there's at least one future segment with a default value. Reporting uses hierarchies instead of hard-coded roll-ups. There's an intercompany segment from day one. Values are consistent globally, with controlled local extensions. There's governance for adding values: owners, approval and naming rules. And it lines up with EPM dimensions and consolidation.\"\n\nTip: Say \"one purpose per segment\". It's the rule that prevents most COA problems.","B"],["Intercompany","How would you design an end-to-end intercompany solution?","**Why they ask:** intercompany crosses procurement, finance, tax and treasury. They want the full picture.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd start by listing the types of flows: goods through supply chain financial orchestration, services and management fees through Fusion Intercompany, financing loans through treasury, and cost allocations through GL allocations with intercompany balancing. The chart has an intercompany segment and balancing rules by legal entity pair. For each country I decide whether intercompany needs a real invoice or just a journal, because tax and statutory rules often require invoices. Then settlement, usually monthly netting through treasury. Then reconciliation, with Fusion intercompany reconciliation or transaction matching in Account Reconciliation. Elimination happens in FCCS. And transfer pricing policy is agreed with tax.\"\n\nTip: Mention invoice vs journal-only by country. It's the decision that tax teams care about.","D"],["Period Close","How would you architect a \"continuous close\" for a large enterprise?","**Why they ask:** a forward-looking architect question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The idea is to do close work every day, not just at month-end. Subledgers account and post continuously, hourly or daily. Reconciliations are automated, with daily bank reconciliations and transaction matching in Account Reconciliation. Accruals and allocations run on schedules. Close tasks are managed in FCCS or Account Reconciliation with dependencies and dashboards. AI anomaly detection flags unusual journals and balances. Monthly is a soft close, with a hard close quarterly where it makes sense. And I'd track close KPIs and improve them every cycle.\"\n\nTip: Open with \"close work every day\". It explains the concept in one line.","D"],["Accounting","How do you design SLA rules for a new revenue stream without disrupting existing accounting?","**Why they ask:** changes to live accounting are risky. They want a safe method.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I identify the event classes and sources the new stream touches. I copy and version the accounting method, and add new journal line rules and account derivation rules with conditions that only fire for the new stream, so existing transactions aren't affected. I use supporting references to report the new stream. I test with an accounting matrix that includes reversals and adjustments. Then I activate it at the start of a period and reconcile the first close carefully.\"\n\nTip: Say \"conditions that only fire for the new stream\". That's how you avoid disrupting existing accounting.","D"],["Enterprise Structures","Design the SCM structure for a company with 5 plants, 12 DCs, central procurement and 3 selling entities.","**Why they ask:** a cross-functional design question that tests whether you understand SCM structures from finance's side.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd create one inventory org per plant and per DC, plus an item master org. Cost orgs by plant using standard cost, and DCs grouped into cost orgs using average or standard, with cost books for primary and management views. Procurement is one central procurement BU, with requisitioning BUs for each operating entity and rules for the sold-to legal entity. Each selling entity gets an order management BU, and fulfillment can come from any DC through sourcing rules. Supply chain financial orchestration handles the flows: central procurement to plants, and plants to selling entities with transfer pricing. Planning is one global supply plan with demand by selling entity or region. Shipping networks connect the DCs for rebalancing with transfer orders. And I'd validate the legal flows and tax with finance before configuring anything.\"\n\nTip: End with \"validate with finance and tax first\". It shows you know where these designs go wrong.","D"],["General Ledger","What are the \"4 Cs\" of a ledger in Oracle?","**Why they ask:** a basic question that every Oracle Financials consultant should answer quickly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A ledger is defined by four things: the chart of accounts, which is the account structure; the calendar, which defines the periods, like monthly or 4-4-5; the currency; and the accounting method, which is the Subledger Accounting method that tells subledgers how to create journals. Two legal entities can share a primary ledger only if all four are the same. If any one differs, they need separate ledgers.\"\n\nTip: Add the rule about sharing a ledger. It turns a definition into a design point.","B"],["General Ledger","What is a balancing segment and why is it important?","**Why they ask:** balancing segments drive entity reporting and intercompany.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The balancing segment is the chart of accounts segment, usually company, for which Oracle makes sure debits equal credits on every journal. If a journal crosses two balancing values, say company 101 and 102, Oracle adds intercompany balancing lines automatically using the intercompany rules. Balancing values are usually assigned to legal entities, so you can produce a balance sheet for each entity. Fusion supports up to three balancing segments, for example company and cost center, if the business needs a balance sheet at that level too.\"\n\nTip: Mention the three balancing segments. It's a Fusion detail that shows current knowledge.","B"],["General Ledger","Walk me through the life of a manual journal entry in Fusion GL.","**Why they ask:** a basic GL question that shows whether you know the controls along the way.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It starts with creating the journal, either on the Create Journal page or by uploading the ADFdi spreadsheet, with batch, journal, ledger, period, category and lines. As lines are entered, cross-validation rules check every account combination and reject invalid ones. If journal approval is on for that source, it routes through the BPM rules, usually by amount. Once approved, it's posted, either by hand or by AutoPost, and posting updates the balances and the cube. If it's an accrual, it's set to reverse automatically in the next period.\"\n\nTip: Mention the spreadsheet upload. Most finance teams enter journals that way, and it shows you've worked with them.","B"],["General Ledger","What are cross-validation rules?","**Why they ask:** a basic COA control that every GL consultant sets up.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Cross-validation rules stop invalid combinations of segment values from being created. For example, 'balance sheet accounts, 1000 to 3999, must use cost center 000'. Each rule has a condition filter, which says when it applies, a validation filter, which says what's allowed, and an error message the user sees. One thing to know: they only stop new combinations. Combinations that already exist have to be disabled separately. So I set them up before transactions start, and test them with real account strings.\"\n\nTip: Make the point that they only block new combinations. It's the follow-up question.","B"],["General Ledger","How do you open and close GL periods, and what statuses can a period have?","**Why they ask:** a basic close question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The statuses are Never Opened, Future Enterable, Open, Closed and Permanently Closed. I open the next period in Manage Accounting Periods or with the Open Period process. In a Future Enterable period, journals can be entered but not posted. I close the period once all subledgers have transferred and all journals are posted and reconciled. A Closed period can be reopened; Permanently Closed can't, so I only use it after audit sign-off. Subledgers like AP, AR and Assets have their own periods, and they close before GL.\"\n\nTip: Explain when you'd use Permanently Closed. It shows care with irreversible steps.","B"],["General Ledger","What is the difference between a primary ledger, a secondary ledger and a reporting currency?","**Why they ask:** a core design question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The primary ledger is the main book of record for a legal entity. A secondary ledger is a parallel ledger that can differ in chart of accounts, calendar, currency or accounting method. It's used for local GAAP vs IFRS or US GAAP, or for a different statutory chart, and it can be kept at subledger, journal or balance level. A reporting currency has the same chart, calendar and method as the primary; only the currency is different. So my rule of thumb: if only the currency differs, it's a reporting currency; if anything else differs, it's a secondary ledger.\"\n\nTip: Give the rule of thumb. It answers the question in one sentence.","D"],["General Ledger","Explain revaluation and translation in GL.","**Why they ask:** people often confuse the two.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Revaluation adjusts foreign-currency balances to the period-end rate. For example, a EUR bank account in a USD ledger is revalued, and the difference goes to unrealized gain or loss, usually reversed next period. Translation converts the whole ledger's balances into another currency for reporting or consolidation: balance sheet at the period-end rate, P&L at the average rate, equity at historical rates, and the difference goes to CTA. Revaluation comes first, after all foreign-currency transactions are posted, then translation.\"\n\nTip: State the order: revalue, then translate. It's what they want to hear.","D"],["General Ledger","How would you set up a recurring allocation, for example rent allocated by headcount?","**Why they ask:** a practical allocation question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In Fusion I'd build it as a Calculation Manager allocation rule. The source, or pool, is the rent account balance for the period. The basis is a statistical account holding headcount by cost center, loaded every month from HR. The target is rent expense on each receiving cost center, in proportion to headcount. The offset is a credit back to the source cost center. It generates each month, on a schedule or by hand, and someone reviews it before posting. In EBS the equivalent is MassAllocations. I test it first with a small set of numbers I can check by hand.\"\n\nTip: Mention testing with numbers you can check by hand. It shows care with allocations.","D"],["Period Close","What steps would you follow to close the Payables period?","**Why they ask:** a practical close question for finance consultants.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make sure all invoices for the period are entered and validated, and holds that matter are resolved. I finish the period's payment runs. I run Create Accounting, transfer to GL, and clear any accounting errors. I review the unaccounted transactions and exception reports. If there are transactions that can't be accounted in time, I sweep them to the next period where that's appropriate. Then I close the Payables period, open the next one, and reconcile the AP trial balance to GL.\"\n\nTip: Mention the sweep option. It shows you've closed a period under pressure.","B"],["Period Close","What is the typical order of closing subledgers before GL?","**Why they ask:** close sequence errors cause rework.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Purchasing and receiving first, so receipts and accruals are complete. Then Payables, with invoices and payments accounted. Then Receivables. Then Cash Management, with bank statements reconciled. Then Assets, which needs Payables done first so additions come through, followed by depreciation. Then Projects, Inventory and Cost if they're used. GL closes last: post, revalue, translate, consolidate and close.\"\n\nTip: Say why Assets follows Payables. Dependencies are the point of the question.","B"],["General Ledger","What are journal categories and sources used for?","**Why they ask:** sources and categories drive approvals, reporting and automation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The journal source says where a journal came from, like Manual, Payables, Receivables, Spreadsheet, or a custom source for an integration. The category says what kind of entry it is, like Accrual, Adjustment, Purchase Invoices or Receipts. They're used in journal approval rules, in reporting and audit, for example showing all manual journals, in reversal options, in AutoPost criteria and in document sequencing.\"\n\nTip: Recommend a custom source per integration. It makes audit and reconciliation much easier.","B"],["General Ledger","What is the difference between Smart View and Financial Reporting Studio?","**Why they ask:** to see if you know which reporting tool to suggest.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Smart View is an Excel add-in that queries the GL balances cube. It's great for ad-hoc analysis, pivoting and building Excel reports with live data and drill-down. Financial Reporting Studio and the Financial Reporting Center produce formatted, governed reports like the P&L and balance sheet for many users, though Oracle is moving those toward Narrative Reporting and Smart View-based reports. Both read the same Essbase cube, which updates when journals post.\"\n\nTip: Mention Oracle's move away from FR Studio. It shows you're current.","D"],["General Ledger","What is the difference between a journal batch, a journal and a journal line?","**Why they ask:** a basic GL structure question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A batch is a group of journals posted together, like one month's accruals. A journal belongs to one ledger, period, category and currency, and must balance. A line is one debit or credit to an account combination, with a description and amount. Approval and posting usually happen at batch level, and reversals can be done per journal.\"\n\nTip: Keep it short and clear. It's a basic question, so a crisp answer is best.","B"],["General Ledger","How do recurring journals work?","**Why they ask:** recurring journals save time every month.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"They're for entries that repeat each period with the same or a calculated amount, like rent, amortization or standard accruals. In Fusion I build them with Calculation Manager formula rules or the recurring journal option, then generate and post them each period. Amounts can be fixed or calculated from balances, for example one-twelfth of a prepaid account. I schedule generation right after the period opens so they're never forgotten.\"\n\nTip: Mention scheduling after period open. It's the practical detail.","B"],["Enterprise Structures","Walk me through how you would design the enterprise structure for a multi-country Fusion Financials implementation.","**Why they ask:** a senior design question. They want a method and building blocks.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start from how the business is organized legally, managerially and for tax, not from the org chart. The building blocks are legal entities, one per registered company; primary ledgers, one per unique combination of the four Cs, where countries that share all four can share a ledger with a balancing value per entity; business units where transactions happen, with service-provider relationships if a shared services center processes for many BUs; reference data sets to share setups like payment terms across BUs; and secondary or reporting currency ledgers for other GAAPs or group currency. My approach is workshops with finance, tax and legal to confirm entities, currencies and statutory needs by country, then design the chart for group consolidation first, with local needs handled by secondary ledgers or mapping. And I prototype in a sandbox with real transactions and a close before locking it in, because this is the hardest thing to change later.\"\n\nTip: Add a real example: how many ledgers, and why you chose secondary ledgers or reporting currencies.","D"],["Chart of Accounts","How do you design a chart of accounts for a global company, and what mistakes have you seen?","**Why they ask:** COA design shapes reporting for years.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A good chart answers reporting questions with segments, not with thousands of natural accounts. Typical segments are company for balancing, cost center, natural account, intercompany, product or line of business, and a future segment, each with a clear owner. Segments should be independent, one meaning each, with no codes that pack two things together. I leave room in number ranges, use hierarchies for roll-ups instead of summary values, and set up cross-validation rules early. The mistakes I see are copying the legacy chart one-for-one with all its workarounds, using cost centers to track projects or customers, having too many segments, and leaving out the intercompany segment, which makes eliminations painful.\"\n\nTip: Give a before-and-after, like cutting 4,000 natural accounts to 900 by moving detail into segments and hierarchies.","D"],["Period Close","How do you run and improve the month-end close in Oracle Financials?","**Why they ask:** every finance leader wants a faster, cleaner close.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I run the close as a sequenced checklist with owners, cut-off times and reconciliations. Procurement and AP first: receipts, accruals, invoices accounted, AP closed. Then AR, then Assets with depreciation, then Cash Management reconciliations. GL last: post journals, revalue and translate, intercompany and allocations, reconcile subledgers to GL, close and consolidate. To improve it, I make status visible with the Close Monitor and task lists, move work earlier with daily accounting and continuous reconciliation, and automate accruals. On one program we went from 8 working days to 5 by automating accruals and bank reconciliation and moving intercompany settlement to weekly.\"\n\nTip: Lead with the number, days to close before and after, then explain what you changed.","D"],["Period Close","A CFO wants to shorten the close from 10 days to 5. What would you do?","**Why they ask:** a business-outcome question. They want a diagnosis first, then levers.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd start by mapping the current close: every task, owner, dependency and duration, to find the critical path. Then I'd pull the levers that shorten that path: continuous accounting with daily Create Accounting, daily bank reconciliation and weekly intercompany settlement; automated accruals, recurring journals and allocations; earlier AP and AR cut-offs with estimates for the last days; automatic reconciliation of balance sheet accounts; a standard close checklist in a task tool so everyone sees status; and fixing data problems upstream so close days aren't spent on corrections. Then I'd measure days to close and post-close adjustments every month and keep improving.\"\n\nTip: Say \"find the critical path first\". Cutting tasks that aren't on it won't shorten the close.","D"],["Period Close","What is a third-party control account in SLA?","**Why they ask:** a key control that keeps subledger and GL in sync.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It's a natural account flagged so that only subledger transactions with a supplier or customer can post to it, like the AP liability or AR receivable account. That stops manual GL journals hitting those accounts, makes third-party balance reports available from SLA, and keeps the subledger-to-GL reconciliation clean. It's set on the natural account value, with Third Party Control Account set to Supplier or Customer.\"\n\nTip: Tie it to reconciliation. That's why controllers want it.","D"],["General Ledger","How do you handle suspense accounts and journal imports from external systems?","**Why they ask:** integrations are a big source of GL errors.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Journals from external systems come in through FBDI with the journal import template, GL REST, or OIC. Each system gets its own journal source for traceability, approval and reporting. Suspense posting can be turned on to accept unbalanced journals, but I'd keep it off and fix imbalances at the source. I validate account combinations before sending, and use dynamic combination creation carefully. Then I monitor import errors every day and reconcile each source system to GL by source.\"\n\nTip: Recommend keeping suspense off. It shows a control mindset.","D"],["General Ledger","What is average daily balancing and when is it used?","**Why they ask:** a specialist question, mostly for banking clients.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Average balance processing keeps daily balances and calculates averages: period to date, quarter to date and year to date. Banks and other financial institutions need it for regulatory reporting and interest calculations. It's enabled on the ledger, and journals then carry effective dates. Fusion supports average daily balances in GL with its own reporting. I'd mention it only if the client is in financial services.\"\n\nTip: If you've worked with a bank or insurer, say so here. It's a good place to show breadth.","D"],["Period Close","How would you use Fusion features to automate intercompany and close tasks?","**Why they ask:** to see if you use what's in the product before building custom tools.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For intercompany: automatic balancing rules, intercompany transactions with automatic invoicing, and the reconciliation reports. For close: allocations and recurring journals in Calculation Manager on a schedule, automatic reversal of accruals, AutoPost criteria, and scheduled revaluation and translation. The Close Monitor gives status, and where available, AI agents give close insights. Together these take a lot of manual steps out of the close.\"\n\nTip: Name what you'd schedule. Scheduling is where most of the time savings come from.","D"],["General Ledger","How do you configure journal approval in Fusion GL?","**Why they ask:** journal approval is a key financial control.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I enable journal approval on the ledger and on the journal sources that need it, like Manual and Spreadsheet. The rules are set in BPM through Manage Journal Approval Rules: by journal amount, for example over $50,000 goes to the controller; by ledger or category; and by supervisor hierarchy or approval groups. I test the edge cases: amounts exactly at the threshold, journals across ledgers, and self-approval prevention. Subledger journals usually don't need approval, because they're controlled upstream.\"\n\nTip: Mention testing at the threshold amount. It's the case that usually breaks.","D"],["General Ledger","What is AutoPost and when would you use it?","**Why they ask:** AutoPost keeps GL current without manual effort.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"AutoPost automatically posts journals that match criteria sets, by ledger, source, category, period and priority. I use it for subledger journals from Payables, Receivables and Assets, and for trusted integrations, so finance doesn't post them by hand. It runs on a schedule. I leave out sources that need review, like manual journals. Combined with Create Accounting's transfer and post options, GL stays current every day.\"\n\nTip: Say which sources you'd exclude. That's the control part of the answer.","D"],["General Ledger","How do segment value security rules work?","**Why they ask:** security design affects users and performance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Segment value security limits which values of a segment a user can see or use, for example a regional accountant can only use cost centers in their region. I enable security on the value set, create data security policies with the value conditions, and grant them through roles in the Security Console. It applies to entry, inquiry and reporting, and works alongside data access sets, which control ledger and balancing segment access. I'm careful with it, because too many rules slow things down and confuse users.\"\n\nTip: Mention the performance cost. It shows you've seen it go wrong.","D"],["General Ledger","What is a data access set?","**Why they ask:** a basic GL security question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A data access set controls which ledgers or ledger sets, and which primary balancing segment values, a user can access in GL, and whether it's read-only or read and write. It can give full ledger access or only specific values, like companies 101 to 110. It's assigned to users with their GL roles through Manage Data Access for Users. With a ledger set, one data access set can cover many ledgers that share a chart and calendar.\"\n\nTip: Give the example of a company range. It makes the answer concrete.","B"],["Intercompany","How does Fusion Intercompany work?","**Why they ask:** intercompany is complex, and Fusion has two parts that people confuse.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"There are two parts. Intercompany balancing is automatic: when a journal crosses balancing values, GL or SLA creates due-to and due-from lines based on the intercompany balancing rules. Intercompany transactions use the Intercompany module: one entity creates a transaction, like a management fee, the partner approves it, and it creates journals in both ledgers and, if needed, AR and AP invoices to settle it. The setup includes intercompany organizations, transaction types, receivables and payables assignments, and the intercompany reconciliation report.\"\n\nTip: Separate the two parts clearly. It's what the question is testing.","D"],["Intercompany","How do you reconcile intercompany balances at month end?","**Why they ask:** intercompany differences delay consolidation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I run the Intercompany Reconciliation report, which compares due-to and due-from between each pair of entities. Then I investigate the differences: timing, where one side booked in the next period; FX rate differences; missing transactions; and manual journals with the wrong intercompany segment. Having an intercompany segment in the chart makes it possible to report balances by trading partner. I agree a cut-off date and a settlement process, usually netting. Eliminations happen in consolidation, in FCCS or with GL elimination rules.\"\n\nTip: List the causes in order of how often you see them. Timing is usually first.","D"],["Budgetary Control","What is budgetary control and encumbrance accounting?","**Why they ask:** important in public sector, higher education and some corporates.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Budgetary control checks spending against budgets in real time. Requisitions, POs and invoices are stopped if they go over the available funds with absolute control, or just warned with advisory control. Encumbrance reserves the funds as spending moves along: a requisition encumbrance, then a PO encumbrance, then the actual on the invoice, with each step relieving the one before. Setup includes control budgets, budgetary control rules, control levels by segment, and loading the budgets. A funds check only validates; a funds reserve actually uses up the funds.\"\n\nTip: Explain funds check vs funds reserve. It's the detail that proves hands-on work.","D"],["Period Close","How do you use the Close Monitor and close tasks in Fusion?","**Why they ask:** close visibility is what controllers want.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The Close Monitor shows period status across subledgers and ledgers in one view, so you can see which are open, closed or pending, and drill into each. Many companies use the task manager in EPM Financial Consolidation and Close, or a tool like BlackLine, for the close checklist, with owners, dependencies and due dates. I set the checklist up to follow the real close sequence, with evidence attached for audit.\"\n\nTip: Mention attaching evidence. Auditors ask for it every quarter.","D"],["Period Close","How do you handle period-end accruals for received but not invoiced (RNI) goods?","**Why they ask:** RNI accruals are a common audit area.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"There are two methods. Accrue at receipt, common for inventory and many expense items, books the accrual when goods are received and clears it when the invoice is matched. Period-end accrual, used for some expense items, runs the accrual process at period end, and the entry reverses next period. Either way, I review the accrual reconciliation report for old or wrong accruals and write them off with approval. Finance decides which method applies by item and destination type.\"\n\nTip: Mention reviewing old accruals. Stale RNI balances are a classic audit finding.","D"],["Multi-currency","How are exchange rates managed and used in Oracle?","**Why they ask:** missing rates block accounting.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"There are rate types, like Corporate, Spot and User. Daily rates are loaded by hand, by spreadsheet, or through an integration from a provider like Reuters, Bloomberg or a bank. Transactions use the rate for their date and rate type, and User rate types let someone type a rate. Revaluation uses period-end rates, and translation uses period-end, average and historical rates. The key operational point is that rates must be loaded before processing, because a missing rate stops accounting.\"\n\nTip: End with the missing-rate point. It's the problem everyone has hit once.","B"],["Multi-currency","How do realized gains and losses arise in AP and AR?","**Why they ask:** a basic multi-currency accounting question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A realized gain or loss happens when a foreign-currency invoice is paid or receipted at a different rate than the invoice rate. For example, a EUR 1,000 supplier invoice booked at 1.10 is $1,100. If we pay it when the rate is 1.12, it costs $1,120, so that's a $20 realized loss. SLA books it to the realized gain or loss account at payment or receipt. Unrealized gains and losses are different: they come from revaluing open balances at period end.\"\n\nTip: Work the example out loud. It makes the answer stick.","B"],["General Ledger","How do you design account hierarchies for reporting?","**Why they ask:** hierarchies drive every GL report.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I build trees for the natural account, assets, liabilities, equity, revenue and expense with sub-levels, and for cost centers, department to division to company. They follow the financial statement lines and management reporting. Tree versions are date-effective; I audit and flatten them, then publish to the Essbase cube. The same hierarchies are used in allocations, Financial Reporting and Smart View. And changes are governed: someone owns the tree, and changes go through change control.\"\n\nTip: Mention publishing to the cube. Forgetting it is why reports don't roll up.","D"],["General Ledger","What is a ledger set and why use one?","**Why they ask:** a basic question about managing many ledgers.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A ledger set groups ledgers that share the same chart of accounts and calendar. With it I can open and close periods for many ledgers at once, run revaluation, translation, allocations and reports across them, and give access with one data access set. The ledgers can have different currencies. It's very useful for companies with many country ledgers.\"\n\nTip: Give a scale example, like closing 20 European ledgers in one step.","B"],["General Ledger","How do you handle a journal posted to the wrong period?","**Why they ask:** a common real-world mistake.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"If the wrong period is still open, I reverse the journal in that same period and re-enter it in the right one. If the wrong period is closed, I reverse it in the current open period, or reopen if policy allows, and post it correctly, with documentation for audit. Then I check whether allocations, revaluation or reports already used the wrong balance. And I fix the cause, often the default period on a spreadsheet upload, or a training gap.\"\n\nTip: Mention checking downstream effects like allocations. It shows thoroughness.","B"],["Accounting","Explain how you would design accounting for a complex revenue arrangement with variable consideration.","**Why they ask:** variable consideration is one of the hardest revenue topics.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under ASC 606 and IFRS 15, variable consideration, like rebates, usage fees or penalties, is estimated using expected value or most likely amount, and constrained so a large reversal isn't likely later. In Oracle, Revenue Management handles allocation and recognition, and variable consideration goes in as estimates that adjust the transaction price, with true-ups as actuals come in. Rebates and chargebacks are handled through channel revenue management or accruals via SLA or GL. The accounting covers contract assets and liabilities, revenue and accrued rebates. Estimates are reviewed every quarter with a documented method, and disclosures cover remaining performance obligations and disaggregation.\"\n\nTip: Explain the constraint. It's the part of 606 interviewers test.","D"],["Accounting","How do you handle Pillar Two / global minimum tax data requirements in the ERP landscape?","**Why they ask:** Pillar Two is a current topic, and tax depends on ERP data.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Pillar Two, the 15% global minimum tax, needs data by jurisdiction: GloBE income, covered taxes and deferred taxes for each constituent entity. The ERP's job is a clean legal entity structure, entity-level trial balances, account mapping to the GloBE adjustments, and intercompany detail. The calculation usually happens in Tax Reporting in EPM or a specialist tax tool fed from GL and FCCS. I'd design data lineage and controls, because tax data is audited too, and work with the tax team early, since changes to the chart or entity mapping may be needed.\"\n\nTip: Say \"work with tax early\". Late changes to entity mapping are expensive.","D"],["Accounting","How would you design accounting for intercompany loans and interest across entities?","**Why they ask:** a treasury and accounting design question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Treasury owns the loan agreements, in a treasury system or in Fusion with a defined process. Interest is accrued monthly by agreement, using the rate and day-count, with journals in both entities, interest income for the lender and expense for the borrower, using the intercompany segment. Foreign-currency loans are revalued, and some may qualify as a net investment. Cross-border interest may need withholding tax. Settlement is by netting or cash. Reconciliation and elimination happen in FCCS. And transfer pricing documentation supports the arm's-length rates.\"\n\nTip: Mention withholding tax and transfer pricing. They're where cross-border loans get complicated.","D"],["Accounting","How would you support IFRS 17 or similar complex accounting standards with Oracle?","**Why they ask:** a specialist question that shows whether you can design for complex standards.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For IFRS 17, the calculations, like the contractual service margin, risk adjustment and cohorts, usually run in actuarial or specialist engines. Fusion Accounting Hub then turns the calculation outputs into accounting under IFRS 17 and local GAAP, with multiple methods and ledgers. I'd design data lineage and reconciliation from policy systems to the engine to Accounting Hub to GL. Detailed dimensions, like portfolio and cohort, go in supporting references rather than COA segments. Disclosures come from a data warehouse. The same pattern works for any complex standard: a specialist calculation, then Accounting Hub, then GL.\"\n\nTip: End with the general pattern. It shows you can apply it beyond insurance.","D"],["Accounting","What is your approach to multi-ledger, multi-currency design for treasury-heavy companies?","**Why they ask:** a senior design question for global companies.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First, the functional currency per entity, decided with accounting under ASC 830 or IAS 21. Reporting currencies where the group needs daily balances in its currency. Revaluation rules by account range, monetary items only, with gain and loss accounts by entity. Hedge accounting runs in the treasury system, with journals to GL, including cash flow hedge reserves in OCI. Translation happens in FCCS with CTA. Daily rates come from a market data provider, with controls on the rates.\"\n\nTip: Say \"functional currency first, decided with accounting\". The design depends on it.","D"],["Accounting","How do you approach a request to restate prior-period accounting in Oracle?","**Why they ask:** restatements are sensitive. They want a careful process.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I confirm the accounting decision with finance and audit, and which periods are affected. The usual option is adjusting entries in the current period; reopening prior periods is possible only if allowed and they haven't been reported. For subledger errors, I correct the source and re-account where possible; otherwise manual journals with clear references. Then I rerun revaluation, translation and consolidation for the affected periods. Everything is documented for audit, and I add a control so it doesn't happen again.\"\n\nTip: Start with \"confirm the accounting decision first\". The system work follows the decision.","D"],["Accounting","How would you design accounting controls around AI-assisted close tasks?","**Why they ask:** AI is entering the close, and auditors will ask how it's controlled.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"AI can prepare accrual proposals, reconciliation match suggestions and variance explanations. People still review and approve, and anything posted goes through normal journal approval. The AI's outputs and traces are attached to the close task as evidence. Review levels are set by materiality thresholds. And internal audit tests the AI-assisted controls like any other control. The principle is that AI prepares and people decide.\"\n\nTip: Close with \"AI prepares, people decide\". It's the line controllers want to hear.","D"]]);
